last weekend we turned our clocks back  an hour and now it is time to also  change your watering clock we check in  with Southern Nevada Water Authority the  sponsors of this segment for more  well as we head into the winter season  there's some things to keep in mind when  it comes to Watering your plants and  your yard and who better to turn to than  our friend Toby bickmore from snwa Toby  let's break it all down what are some  things to keep in mind as we head into  this new season when it comes to those  mandatory watering times so starting  November 1st we're in Winter watering  schedule so it's November 1st through  the end of February which basically  means you can only water one day a week  and so that's the big thing doesn't  matter what type of landscaping you have  you can only water one day a week if you  have grass you know one day a week is  enough you know especially with the  nights cooling off and everything you  know it's okay for the plants other  types of plants in the winter time you  can probably water you know one to two  times like over a couple of weeks you  don't have to water as much we always  say the other thing in the winter time  is water a little bit later in the day  so don't water super early we do get  kind of cold in the you know in the  January time and so you don't want to  have water freezing and things like that  so  um obviously with the situation we have  going on with the water right now and  the drought we're going to go into tier  two shortages next year we're gonna have  more Cuts so it's just really important  that we follow those watering schedules  throughout the year yeah and as you  mentioned it is very important to follow  those schedules but I think a lot of us  are wondering how to even figure that  out well how do we figure out our day  what advice you have for people as  they're getting ready to set those  clocks for the new season so our website  is a great resource so if you don't know  we send a lot of mail that says this is  your watering day but if you're not sure  visit our website snwa.com and you can  look up your address and you can find  out you know what day a week to water  and so you know that's just a real good  tool to use you know for resources and  you touched on it a little bit but once  you figure out your day and what's  allowed how do you gauge how much how  little where do you go with that when it  comes to your own personal watering yeah  so for for grass usually the general  idea is you're going to water three  times for about four or five minutes  right and so that's anybody has Turf  it's kind of the same philosophy across  the board if you have Desert Landscaping  it's a little bit different because you  can have plants that are really desert  type and some that are higher water use  but generally speaking you're going to  water just once not three times and  you're gonna water anywhere from like 15  minutes to 30 to 40 minutes and again  maybe once a week but when it gets  really cold maybe once every two weeks  all right well this is really helpful  and we are all about conserving water  and money so any other tips that you  have for us at home as we head into the  new season yeah so there's a couple of  things so the the biggest thing we have  right now is if you have grass that you  don't use take that grass out we provide  a rebate for it but grass uses the most  water which means it's going to cost you  the most in your bill the other thing is  watch Water waste so you know you can  check on other people too in the  community you know see if anybody else  is wasting water but you're yourself if  you're losing water out on the street if  you have leaks you know that's water  that you're paying for so definitely  look at that and then we have some other  rebates on our website snwa.com one of  them is actually an irrigation  controller that runs all your  landscaping that's what we call Smart  and so it actually can save you some  money too  thank you to Southern Nevada Water  Authority for sponsoring this segment  call  702-258 save or visit  snwa.com for more information
 

